  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Quiet Desk",
    "overview": "The chrome is a working desk set back from the work. It is neutral grey in dark mode (the default) and plain white in light. Provident Navy runs through it as one family of tints. The only pictures are the posts' own 3:4 renders. The room stays neutral because the studio's job is judging colour on a canvas, so the chrome never competes with it. The home screen follows one rule: one decision at a time. It shows the posts that wait on you or a new post, behind one pill switch, and never stacks a form, a gallery and a list on one page.\n\nDensity is moderate. The rows are full width, with a render, a name, a step line, and one action at the row's end. Hierarchy comes from weight (500 for names, 600 for headings and buttons, 400 for everything else) and from three ink tiers (ink, ink-2, dim). Size does very little of the work. Nothing is bold and nothing is uppercase. Copy is sentence case and plain (\"Waiting on you\", \"You're clear\", \"Send to copy check\").",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Neutral grey ground in dark mode and white in light. Navy is never the ground.",
      "One solid navy action per row. Choices sit on a quiet resting tint.",
      "Rings, not shadows. A card is a fill plus a 1px inset hairline.",
      "The four workflow steps show as small segments coloured link, warn and good.",
      "Google Sans Flex only, with the hierarchy carried by weight and ink tier.",
      "Motion is short and ease-out, and the reduced-motion setting turns it off."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Loud Thing Rule",
        "body": "Each row has at most one solid navy button, and it is the step that moves the post on. Open, Refresh and every secondary action are quiet: ghost, outline or text link.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Rest Is Not Action Rule",
        "body": "A selected state uses the resting tint (`resting-navy`). The solid navy is reserved for doing something. When the two are mixed, the screen loses its one call to action.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Two Systems Rule",
